Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Locomotives Rust Upward
- Story & Context: When an Arctic squall froze the switching points at Thurso Station in December 1998, signalman Callum Henderson bypassed dead telegraph wires by tapping into an illicit polar lighthouse transmission drifting down from the Cape Wrath light tower. Instead of naval alerts, the emergency platform tannoy blasted skittering 135-BPM garage breaks and an unearthly Theremin cry over the scent of spilled Earl Grey tea and frozen diesel fuel. Stranded passengers and shivering rail crews stopped cursing the delays, huddling beneath the station clock to chant the acoustic chorus hook looping through the horn speakers: "We missed the seven-twenty, but the salt don't care about time." Within a week, the clandestine broadcast was dubbed onto hundreds of Maxwell tapes swapped across northern rail yards.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A desolate, frost-covered British railway platform at midnight under an amber station lamp, an abandoned maroon locomotive engine encrusted in sea rime on the tracks, the glow of an ancient signal box in the distance, cinematic 35mm grain, moody teal and rusted orange palette, lyric text ghosted in vapor: "We missed the seven-twenty, but the salt don't care about time."

Track 2: Did You Catch the 04:15?
- Story & Context: Ollie wrote this devastating confession inside a shattered payphone booth at Aberdeen Central, scratching chord charts onto the cardboard back of a discarded timetable while freezing slush soaked through his suede trainers. He had just walked out on his partner after she threatened to dismantle the rogue VHF transmitter he used to bounce pirate garage beats back to isolated keepers in the North Sea. The song pairs an agonizingly slow 12-string acoustic strum with sudden, violent grime sub-bass glides and an expressive Theremin that mirrors the Doppler wail of an engine departing into the darkness: "You wanted wallpaper and quiet Sundays, but I only hear the buoy bell in the bass." Captured on a portable cassette deck with a wool scarf used as a pop shield, the take preserves the sound of the final northbound sleeper car pulling away forever.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Looking out from inside a rain-streaked yellow payphone booth across an empty wet train depot at dawn, blurred silhouettes of departing passenger carriages, a solitary figure in a soaked track jacket looking back, sodium vapor lighting casting deep reflections on wet tarmac, poetic melancholy, cinematic realism.

Track 3: Muckle Skerry Halt
- Story & Context: Myth surrounds the recording of the EP's final piece, which took place when an off-duty diver named Einar accidentally dropped a hydrophone into the freezing bilge waters beneath an abandoned island rail pier facing the Pentland Firth. The microphone captured the distant horn of an incoming cargo vessel tuned to the exact pitch of Maya Lind's Theremin, which collided unexpectedly with a pre-programmed, syncopated 138-BPM grime square-bass pattern blasting from the keeper's quarters above. Local boatmen still swear that on pitch-black February nights, ships steering past the rocky skerries pick up Ollie's tape-saturated vocal floating across their radar monitors: "Every timetable's an anchor until the tide takes the rails." The original master cassette was discovered months later, pickled inside a sealed paraffin canister left on an empty baggage trolley.
